Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Articular)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Transient joint pain without deformity, mild fatigue, manageable with hydroxychloroquine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-045
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, migrating joint pain and profound fatigue requiring exclusion of acute systemic lupus crisis or acute viral polyarthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate rapid onset of severe, multi-joint pain and intense fatigue in a patient with known lupus. Immediate clinical review was mandatory to differentiate a localized articular flare from a life-threatening systemic crisis (e.g., lupus nephritis, pericarditis) or acute septic arthritis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My joints are screaming in pain, it's moving from my wrists to my knees, and I am so exhausted I physically cannot stand up.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a history of non-erosive lupus arthritis. Today, the patient experienced an acute escalation of transient joint pain without deformity, accompanied by profound, debilitating mild fatigue, manageable historically with hydroxychloroquine. The pain is severe, migrating between the wrists, MCP joints, and bilateral knees. The profound lethargy has left the patient bedbound this evening, triggering severe anxiety regarding major organ failure or a systemic flare. Denies chest pain, shortness of breath, gross hematuria, or new facial rashes.
Functional Status: Markedly reduced, currently remaining in bed due to fatigue and joint pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, acute pain and fatigue limiting self-care tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, appearing acutely fatigued, moving deliberately to avoid joint pain. No acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse tenderness over the bilateral wrists, MCP joints, and knees. Joints are tender to palpation and painful on active range of motion, but completely lack severe, tense effusions, extreme localized heat, or intense erythema. Cardiovascular exam reveals normal heart sounds without friction rubs (no pericarditis). Respiratory exam is clear (no pleuritis). Abdomen is soft. No active malar rash or oral ulcers.
Environmental Observations: House is tidy; patient has access to water by the bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes regular hydroxychloroquine but missed doses over the last week.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Articular and Constitutional Flare of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Mild Articular) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Lupus Pericarditis/Pleuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of pleuritic chest pain, normal respiratory rate, and no auscultated friction rubs.

Acute Septic Polyarthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Migratory nature of the pain without tense, bright red, exquisitely hot joints, and absence of high fever or rigors.

Acute Viral Infection (e.g., Parvovirus/Influenza) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of upper respiratory symptoms, true high fever, or sick contacts.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (living alone with severe fatigue and pain)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe migratory polyarthralgia and profound fatigue, raising clinical concern for a severe systemic lupus flare involving major organs. Triage appropriately escalated the call to perform a comprehensive physical examination. Bedside evaluation firmly established normal hemodynamics, clear lung fields, and absence of pericardial rubs, isolating the physical signs to a diffuse, non-critical articular and constitutional flare. This assessment definitively ruled out life-threatening organ involvement and safely averted an inappropriate late-night hospital admission for a patient who primarily required targeted analgesia, rest, and reassurance, satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs (e.g., naproxen 500mg) for acute joint pain and instructed on resuming regular hydroxychloroquine doses immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change: Take the prescribed anti-inflammatory with a full glass of water and immediately use a warm wheat pack on your most painful joints. By strictly restarting your baseline medication and utilizing local heat therapy, you will rapidly suppress the joint inflammation and drastically improve your mobility by morning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Urgent GP or rheumatology review within 48-72 hours to assess flare resolution and discuss medication adherence.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sharp pain in your chest when breathing in, severe shortness of breath, a high fever, or if your urine turns the color of cola or tea.

